ip showed that in terms of the um  the question of visas and ukrainians the  home secretary has uh set out the plans  for a bespoke humanitarian um scheme for  ukraine allowing up to a hundred  thousand to come here the dependence of  british nationals in the uk the  dependence of uh ukrainians living in  this country  and i think that's the right way to  approach and it follows uh the wider  action how does the 75 year old woman  sorry sorry to interrupt you but how  does the sirens just going off here but  how does a 75 year old woman living with  sirens going off living in a basement  get to her son who's living in london  can she do that is she able to do that  because i can't see how she does how  does she go to la vie of 11 hours drive  away get a visa and then it just doesn't  make sense i don't see how she  can get to her son in london  under the current system well  this is a a consistent feature whenever  we have humanitarian asylum schemes set  up we know that the difficulties on the  ground make it practically very  challenging  but we also know that many will uh get  out of ukraine those that uh uh fleeing  through fear uh and we need to be ready  with our international partners the  europeans uh others around you who are  saying anybody any ukraine can come in  any ukrainian come in for up to three  years or certainly extended family  members other countries are doing why  why are we not doing more  i mean that  we we've set out about the 75 year old  woman is a real one yes but i can't  comment on every uh potential  hypothetical case what i've just set out  is the circumstances and of course we  understand the difficulties of the  practical situation on the ground that's  common to most uh uh  conflict zones of course and we  recognize that we'll work with all of  our international partners and the un uh  to try and resolve that and to to to  make sure that we continue this  tradition of of of offering haven to  safe haven to those fleeing persecution  in relation to the europeans of course  they're closer to ukraine um uh and uh  as the ukrainian ambassador the uk said  over the weekend uh it it will often be  the case that  first priority most ukrainians want to  stay in ukraine but if they leave they  want to be as close to their their home  country as possible uh in order to be  able to come back at some point in the  future so i think i don't i think this  woman wants to get to see her son in  london but you know i mean she doesn't  want to go to poland to stay in poland  she wants to go and see her son in  london  in fact the case but it's clear from  what the home secretary announced  yesterday that that we would make that  uh route technically open of course it's  very difficult to know how exactly she  would um traverse the country at that  age  but but that is the offer that home  secretary has made so actually it  directly addresses the scenario you've  described  okay uh dominic rob i appreciate you  being with us we're  slightly distracted here by sirens but  listen i appreciate your  talking to us this morning good to talk  to you

